WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.780
With its ever-expanding selection of films,
Netflix has plenty of psychological thrillers

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:06.140
that are perfect for movie night.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.360
But it's time to look beyond popular films
like Bird Box and No Country for Old Men.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.880
Here are a few disturbing films you probably
haven't seen ... but don't watch them alone!

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.480
In The Good Neighbor, we immediately learn
that the supposed haunting of Harold Grainey

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:24.500
— played by James Caan — has been orchestrated
by his two tech-savvy teenage neighbors, Sean

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:25.140
and Ethan.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.540
But that doesn't make the situation any less
terrifying.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.520
"With the proper execution, it is possible
to drive an unsuspecting person into believing

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.980
that they are truly being haunted."

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:38.360
Sean has his reservations about the whole
scheme, but Ethan is clearly on a mission.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.850
For this guy, getting the reclusive old man
to believe he's being haunted is more than

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:42.500
just a prank.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.960
The reason he's tormenting Grainey is far
darker than that… and the consequences turn

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:47.880
out to be deadly.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.960
Grainey handles the strange occurrences in
his home surprisingly well… until Ethan

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.280
escalates his efforts and pushes him over
the brink.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:58.700
This interesting take on the found-footage
genre serves as a warning about the many pitfalls

00:00:58.700 --> 00:00:59.440
of revenge.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:00.740
"Is he looking at us?

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.000
He knows!"

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:06.020
Simply put, The Good Neighbor is one of the
most intense thrillers Netflix has to offer

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:06.680
right now.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.140
Brick might not be as well known as Joseph
Gordon-Levitt's other films, but there's a

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.530
reason it's become a cult favorite.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.560
In this twisty mystery, Gordon-Levitt portrays
Brendan Frye, a lonely teenager who recently

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.740
went through a breakup with his ex-girlfriend,
Emily — played by Emilie de Ravin of Lost

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:22.240
fame.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.940
When Brendan finds a note directing him to
a pay phone, he's already in way over his

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:26.440
head.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.460
Emily winds up dead, and Brendan starts working
his way through a mystery that involves a

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:34.921
musclebound bully, a high school drug kingpin,
and a few seemingly random words that serve

00:01:34.921 --> 00:01:36.880
as cryptic clues to Emily's fate.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.980
Needless to say, it's all very hardboiled.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.180
"1250, Vista Blanca, the ink blotter on the
desk, in the den, in the basement of the house

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:44.630
with the tacky mailbox."

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:49.210
Directed by Rian Johnson, Brick pays homage
to classic detective films, but the plot and

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:50.820
characters are still distinctly modern.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.840
Although Gordon-Levitt has certainly appeared
in bigger blockbusters, it'd be a real shame

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:55.720
to overlook Brick.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:00.429
With its clever plotting, rapid-fire dialogue,
and sly references to film noir classics,

00:02:00.429 --> 00:02:03.540
this psychological thriller is definitely
worth investigating.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.840
What's scarier than suddenly discovering there's
an uninvited guest in your house?

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.540
We've seen this plot play out in horror films
a million times before… but we've never

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.000
seen a film approach the premise the way that
Hush does.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:18.340
Kate Siegel plays Maddie, a deaf-mute woman
who lost her ability to hear and speak as

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.020
a teenager.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.140
These days, she lives alone in an isolated
cabin in the woods.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.480
However, her quiet life takes a turn for the
terrifying when a masked intruder begins to

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.160
prey upon her.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.500
When he realizes Maddie can't hear, the psychopath
uses his newfound knowledge to torment his

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.280
new victim... and what follows is a fiendish
game of cat-and-mouse that simply won't let

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:44.780
up.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:48.540
Hush is a serious nail-biter that's decidedly
not for the faint of heart.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:53.940
The found-footage film Creep is indeed a full-fledged
creepshow — and it comes highly recommended.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.700
The story begins innocently enough for our
protagonist Aaron.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.590
The videographer is heading out to meet a
new client named Josef, who wants to create

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.380
a series of videos for his unborn son.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.439
Josef tells Aaron that he's dying of cancer,
but he wants his son to have a way of getting

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:07.120
to know his father.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:11.860
But as Josef's behavior becomes stranger and
more concerning, Aaron has a strong urge to

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:12.360
flee.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.840
After all, Josef's stories aren't adding up,
and Aaron is worried for his own safety.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.280
But Josef is determined to keep tabs on Aaron,
and when the videographer runs, he quickly

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.280
discovers that he can't really hide from this
full-fledged psycho.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:26.200
"Are you gonna let me go?"

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.800
And if you're looking for a truly chilling
"Netflix and chill" experience, you're in

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:34.300
luck.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:39.000
In 2017, the sequel Creep 2 was released,
and it's also streaming on Netflix.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:40.220
Double the derangement!

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:45.980
In Backtrack, Adrien Brody plays Peter Brower,
a psychotherapist who's starting to realize

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.620
there's something strange about his clients.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.020
Like, seriously strange.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.900
Meanwhile, Peter is suffering recurring nightmares
and depressive episodes due to the death of

00:03:58.900 --> 00:03:59.400
his daughter.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.260
Eventually, he comes to a somewhat startling
revelation: All of his clients are dead.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.320
If only he'd picked up on the subtle signs
sooner...

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:17.000
Peter begins to research who his clients were
when they were alive, and eventually discovers

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.430
they all lived near a specific train line
— one that happened to regularly pass right

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:21.300
through his hometown.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:26.200
Now, Peter must retrace his steps by going
back home, seeking out some familiar faces,

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.620
and trying to understand why he's at the center
of a haunting.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:32.620
Backtrack is a journey through Peter's murky
memory as he struggles to figure out how to

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.120
make things right between his past and his
present.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:39.570
This psychological thriller serves as a strong
reminder that Adrien Brody is a singular talent

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:42.820
— and he turns in an absolutely fantastic
performance here.

